From f0cc6dc5427e7b5cbad72c9b4b201a0569073779 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 11:06:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 176/294] gallium/pvr: the fragment static stream now matches
 the working driver byte-for-byte

  csb: STATIC STREAM MATCHES THE CAPTURE BYTE-FOR-BYTE - 384 bytes, an external reference
  --- ALL PASS        (real open kernel)

The first fully external verification in this programme: the stream this driver builds is byte-identical
to the one the working Vulkan driver submitted, captured earlier with PVR_CSB_DUMP.

Three things closed since round 82, in order:
  - the "four missing dwords" were trailing ZERO PADDING; the stream is padded to 384;
  - my PVR_CSB_DUMP hexdump stopped at 256 bytes, so the "last two dwords" I had been reasoning about
    were the last two of a truncated dump. Three rounds of theory came from that. A truncated instrument
    is worse than none;
  - the EOT packet's field is the raw PDS offset, so the reference's value 0x2780 is its pixel-event
    program offset - not the 0x20 I had inferred from the same truncated dump.

Also lands PVR_CSB_TAIL (dumps length, header word and the last eight dwords at submit time) - the
instrument that actually answered it, in one run, after three rounds of reading could not.

Padded to a 32-byte boundary, noted as inferred from ONE capture: a second capture with a different
attachment count would distinguish "align to 32" from "align to 16 plus a fixed 16".
---
 src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_csb.c             | 11 +++-
 src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_probe.c           | 52 ++++++++++++-------
 src/imagination/vulkan/pvr_arch_job_render.c  | 12 +++++
 .../winsys/powervr/pvr_drm_job_render.c       | 16 +++++-
 4 files changed, 68 insertions(+), 23 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_csb.c b/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_csb.c
index 9a31b97d..cacfd7f0 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_csb.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_csb.c
@@ -423,8 +423,15 @@ pvr_frag_static_stream(void *stream, uint32_t stream_size, const uint64_t *pbe_w
     * EOT packet is PACKED, not the end of the stream - so the EOT packet's own length has to be added
     * for the total. Omitting it made the builder return one dword less than its own macros summed to,
     * which is what round 78 mistook for a two-number disagreement. */
-   const uint32_t total_dwords = PVR_FRAG_PRE_PBE_DWORDS + pbe_dwords + tail_dwords +
-                                 PVR_CMD_DW(CR_EVENT_PIXEL_PDS_DATA);
+   const uint32_t used_dwords = PVR_FRAG_PRE_PBE_DWORDS + pbe_dwords + tail_dwords +
+                                PVR_CMD_DW(CR_EVENT_PIXEL_PDS_DATA);
+   /* ponytail: pad the stream to a 32-byte boundary. The captured working stream is 384 bytes where the
+    * packets account for 368, i.e. four zero dwords of padding - so the emitted stream is aligned, not
+    * exactly the packet sum. INFERRED FROM ONE CAPTURE: one data point cannot distinguish "align to 32"
+    * from "align to 16 then add a fixed 16". A second capture with a different attachment count would
+    * settle it; until then this matches the only reference there is, and the check asserts the padding is
+    * zero rather than assuming it. */
+   const uint32_t total_dwords = ALIGN_POT(used_dwords, 8);
    const uint32_t total_bytes = PVR_DW_TO_BYTES(total_dwords);
    const uint32_t pbe_byte_off = PVR_DW_TO_BYTES(PVR_FRAG_PRE_PBE_DWORDS);
    /* tail_dwords stops AT the EOT packet's slot (the offset helper returns where it is packed), so the
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_probe.c b/src/gallium/drivers/pvr/pvr_probe.c
